    kalkSelvkostResInklEq: '= Selling price incl. VAT',
    kalkSelvkostNote: 'All amounts are excl. VAT. VAT is added to the selling price at the end.',
    kalkSelvkostFormelToggle: 'Show formula',
    kalkSelvkostF1: 'Materials add-on = Direct materials × materials rate%',
    kalkSelvkostF2: 'Cost of manufacture = Direct materials + materials add-on + direct labour + machine/other + manufacturing add-on',
    kalkSelvkostF3: 'Full cost = Cost of manufacture + (Cost of manufacture × selling/admin rate%)',
    kalkSelvkostF4: 'Selling price excl. VAT = Full cost × (1 + profit%) · incl. VAT = × 1.25',
    kalkSelvkostFNote: '“Full cost is the price floor in the long run — it covers every cost, and it is used for inventory measurement (IAS 2). Use contribution / contribution margin for short-term decisions.”',
    kalkSelvkostGuideKicker: 'How to calculate',
    kalkSelvkostGuideTitle: 'From materials to price — step by step',
    kalkSelvkostStep1: '<b>Direct costs first.</b> Materials, labour and machine hours you can attribute straight to the product — plus a materials add-on for purchasing and waste.',
    kalkSelvkostStep2: '<b>Cost of manufacture.</b> Add a manufacturing add-on as a % of direct labour. It covers indirect production costs, such as rent and power for the workshop. The sum is what the product costs to make.',
    kalkSelvkostStep3: '<b>Full cost.</b> A selling & administrative add-on as a % of the cost of manufacture covers the rest of the business. Finally you add profit on top of full cost.',
    kalkSelvkostTableTitle: 'The example as a table',
    kalkSelvkostColLedd: 'Line',
    kalkSelvkostColGrunnlag: 'Basis',
    kalkSelvkostColBelop: 'Amount',
    kalkSelvkostRow2Basis: '6 % of materials',
    kalkSelvkostRow5Basis: '120 % of labour',
    kalkSelvkostTvkTitle: 'Cost of manufacture',
    kalkSelvkostSumLbl: 'sum',
    kalkSelvkostRow7Basis: '18 % of cost of manufacture',
    kalkSelvkostRow9Basis: '15 % of full cost',
    kalkSelvkostRow10Basis: 'full cost × 1.15',
    kalkSelvkostWhoKicker: 'Who uses it',
    kalkSelvkostWhoTitle: 'Three typical cases',
    kalkSelvkostWho1Lbl: 'Manufacturer',
    kalkSelvkostWho1Desc: 'A full costing per batch to make sure every product covers its share of running the business.',
    kalkSelvkostWho2Lbl: 'Workshop',
    kalkSelvkostWho2Desc: 'Materials plus machine hours and labour — the manufacturing add-on picks up the workshop\'s fixed costs.',
    kalkSelvkostWho3Lbl: 'Tradesperson',
    kalkSelvkostWho3Desc: 'Knows the price floor before the quote goes out — full cost is the lowest price that pays off over time.',
    kalkSelvkostFaqQ1: 'What is the difference between cost of manufacture and full cost?',
    kalkSelvkostFaqA1: 'Cost of manufacture is what it costs to make the product — materials, labour and production costs. Full cost adds selling and administration, and therefore covers every cost in the business.',
    kalkSelvkostFaqQ2: 'When should I use full cost rather than contribution margin?',
    kalkSelvkostFaqA2: 'Full cost is the price floor in the long run — it has to be covered for the business to pay off. For short-term decisions, such as taking an extra order with spare capacity, use contribution margin instead.',
    kalkSelvkostFaqQ3: 'Is full cost used for inventory measurement?',
    kalkSelvkostFaqA3: 'No, not all of it. Inventory is measured at cost of manufacture (IAS 2). The selling and administrative add-on is normally kept out of the inventory value and expensed in the period.',
    kalkSelvkostRel1Desc: 'Set the right price from the cost of goods, and see the difference between markup and contribution margin ratio.',
    kalkSelvkostRel2Desc: 'Short-term price floor — what do the variable costs cover?',
    kalkSelvkostRel3Desc: 'From the salary you want to what you actually have to invoice per hour.',
    kalkEnhetskostTitle: 'Unit cost',
    kalkEnhetskostSubtitle: 'Cost per unit — even when the variants differ in how demanding they are.',
    kalkEnhetskostStatKost: 'Cost per converted unit',
    kalkEnhetskostStatEkv: 'Converted units',
